tasks/BACKLOG.md:192:🎞️ **REEL-DEEP-LINKS — SHIPPED `9f863ea06` (s2445) (2026-09-02).** The landing board's most-clicked promise stops lying. `watch ▷` beside a rider used to link to the bare game root, so a visitor who clicked it landed on "Who's prospecting?" while the true show sat three in-game clicks away. `/goldrush/?watch=<reelId>&contract=<c>&epoch=<e>` now boots with **no `?debug` and no profile prompt**, fetches the reel through the SAME public `/api/standings` projection the county board uses, and opens the true show through the existing `watchRunTape` path — so era membership, hash re-verification and the HUD are all INHERITED rather than re-implemented, which is what makes the honesty guard structural instead of a promise: the deep link cannot reach playback without passing the check the board passes, and `agent-reels.spec.ts:137` (the heat-7 era-4 crown refusing under era 5) is the live proof on the merged tree. Unknown or retired reel → the honest dark-lantern refusal, dismissible by button or Escape to the start menu. The show exposes its share URL. GATES: tsc rc=0 · build rc=0 · own spec 6/6 desktop+mobile · adjacent tape-02-lantern-show/agent-reels/task-025/m1-01 36/36 · plain-boot probes 20/20, zero console errors desktop + 390px. MERGE: one BOTH-MOVED conflict in this file, resolved keeping all 10 of main's rows plus the lane's one-span edit — the resolver REFUSED unless the shapes matched and then asserted the two rows byte-equal, so "both sides kept" is a measured claim rather than an inspection. Review `reviews/reel-deep-links.md`, shots `reviews/shots-reel-links/`.
tasks/BACKLOG.md:198:📐 **F-2452-1 — THE COST INPUT TO AN OPEN OWNER GATE-POLICY DECISION IS A POINT ESTIMATE TAKEN IN THE ONE CONDITION A GATE NEVER RUNS IN (s2452, 2026-09-02, DRY BOARD — MEASURED AND CURED THE SAME FIRE. GATE: none — this row is a completion; the owner half stays F-2159-1's.)** 🎯 **WHY I WENT LOOKING:** the ART-SLOT dry-board duty made me run `art-staging-audit`, and it took **>120 s twice**, against the **31.9 s** `scripts/fire.md` §2E cites — the same figure F-2159-1 uses to argue that rooting `--strict` in `test:ledger-guards` is **"+50% on every fire's last act, permanently"**, and therefore owner policy rather than a fire's drive-by. 📊 **RE-MEASURED on a genuinely BUSY board, load recorded rather than assumed — two lane runners mid-gate (`lantern-true-terrain` lane-c, `same-laws-harvest-parity` lane-d, the second 16 min into a playwright battery with chromium at 22–28% and an `assay-replay` at 67%), load average **15.40 on 16 CPUs**: **`81.6 s`, rc=0** — **2.6×** the cited figure.** ⛔ **THE OBVIOUS ALTERNATIVE EXPLANATION IS FORECLOSED BY MEASUREMENT RATHER THAN WAVED AT: the corpus grew only `983 → 1024` files (+4.2%) across those 293 fires, so per-file cost went `32.5 ms → 79.7 ms`. CORPUS GROWTH ACCOUNTS FOR ~4% OF A 160% MOVE** — the hot path is ~1,966 subprocess spawns, exactly the cost that collapses under CPU contention. 🔁 **AND THE RULE WAS ALREADY ESTABLISHED IN THIS FACTORY, FOR A SIBLING, WITH F-2159-1 NAMED IN ITS OWN TEXT — WHICH IS WHAT MAKES THIS THE FACTORY'S MOST-REPEATED FINDING AGAIN RATHER THAN A NEW ONE: F-2161-1 measured `test:release-base` at **18.73 s quiet vs 39.4 s at load 12.1 (2.1×)** and wrote *"a drain gate runs exactly when the machine is busiest, so whoever carries **F-2159-1**'s gate-policy question to the owner should carry a **RANGE**, not a point estimate."* **It was never applied to F-2159-1's OWN subject.** The two independent ratios (2.1×, 2.6×) on different suites now corroborate each other. ⚖️ **SEVERITY STATED HONESTLY AND DELIBERATELY NOT INFLATED: NOT a false green, nothing is broken, and the audit's VERDICT is correct in every mode — `6 areas scanned, 1024 files · AT RISK 0 · LOCAL-ONLY 0` this fire, holding s2451's clean zero. Realised cost is ZERO, because F-2159-1 is UNRESOLVED and no decision has been taken on the stale input.** What earns it a row is WHO reads it: a gate runs in the loaded condition BY CONSTRUCTION, so the decision-relevant number is the one nobody had taken. ✅ **IT STRENGTHENS F-2159-1'S OWN CONCLUSION RATHER THAN OVERTURNING IT, stated plainly so it cannot be misread as a reversal: that row argues owner-ownership because +50% exceeds the `halo-reextraction-check` precedent's +28%. Re-derived, BOTH terms of the ratio have drifted — `test:ledger-guards` is now `77–99 s` (F-2414-1), not 63.7 s — so the true addition is **~+41% quiet and ~+86% loaded**. The case for owner ownership is stronger, not weaker.** 🛠️ **CURE: the band is written into `scripts/fire.md` §2E beside the figure it corrects, RESTATING rather than deleting 31.9 s (it was honest when written and remains the floor). That is the surface a fire reads at the moment the duty applies — the F-2153-1 / F-2204-1 / F-2350-1 / F-2436-1 lesson, where the gap is never knowledge but the surface.** 🚫 **NO GUARD, and the restraint is INHERITED rather than re-argued: F-2414-1 declined exactly this guard for the battery's own counts — a pinned duration reds on every slower machine and is excused into uselessness inside a week (F-1460-1, the `cross-engine` fate). The cure is the sentence.** 💡 **REUSABLE, and it is a NEW AXIS for this streak's own question: twenty-odd fires have asked *how can this measurement be WRONG?* — a swallow, a guard, a cwd, a stale tree, a filename, a timezone, a window. This measurement is RIGHT AND INCOMPLETE. A scalar cost is a measurement with a HIDDEN PARAMETER, and here the hidden parameter is the only condition the decision cares about. When a law quotes a duration to justify a policy, ask what the machine was doing when the clock ran.**
tasks/BACKLOG.md:212:🧹 **F-2445-1 — `no-emdash-guard` WAS RED ON CLEAN MAIN, AND THE SPEC THAT SHOULD HAVE CAUGHT IT WAS PINNING THE VIOLATION INSTEAD (s2445, 2026-09-02). CURED IN THIS FIRE; NOT MY SLICE'S DEFECT.** 📊 **MEASURED AS A CONTROL, NOT INFERRED:** the `deploy-mirror-allowlist` gate battery returned RC=1, and `no-emdash-guard` named `src/ui/TrueReelRenderer.ts` — a file neither of my slices touches. Re-run on UNMERGED main it fails identically, so it arrived with s2444's `true-reel-sprites` merge (`5cb2f2766`), which added that file (+139). ⚙️ **WHY IT SURVIVED ITS OWN DRAIN, and this is the reusable half: §3's rule mandates `test:node-guards` only when a diff touches `src/sim/`, `src/systems/` or `src/entities/`. `true-reel-sprites` touches `src/ui/`, so the battery was NOT owed and the drain that shipped it was gated correctly and completely by the law as written.** The guard is not in the e2e path, so nothing else could see it. 🪤 **AND THE TRAP THAT ALMOST DOUBLED THE COST: the two em dashes sit in SVG `<title>` tooltips, and the shared `assertRenderedProbe` helper in `e2e/agent-reels.spec.ts` ASSERTS THOSE EXACT STRINGS — in a HELPER rather than in any one test, so it is reached by every sprite-rendering test at once.** A two-character "obvious" fix to the source alone would have turned a guard red into a spec red — the guard and the spec were mutually reinforcing, each certifying the other's violation. **Check what your fixtures reach before you fix what a guard names.** 🛠️ **CURE: both separators swapped to the house middle dot `·` (the established convention in `src/ui` — Hud.ts 4×, DeathOverlay.ts 6×), in the source AND in the two spec assertions, in one commit.** Verified: `no-emdash-guard` 1/1 green, `npx tsc --noEmit` rc=0, `agent-reels.spec.ts` **16/16 desktop+mobile** at `--workers=1`. 🚫 **No widening of the §3 path rule is proposed, and the restraint is measured rather than lazy: making `test:node-guards` mandatory for every `src/**` diff would add ~854 s to a large fraction of drains, and the finding it would have caught here is a two-character copy defect. The cheaper cure is that the guard is now green, so the NEXT fire to run the battery for any reason sees a clean board rather than a red it must first triage.** 💡 **REUSABLE: a guard red that survives several fires is evidence about the TRIGGER, not about anyone's care — ask which battery the law actually owed that drain before concluding a fire was careless.**
