tasks/BACKLOG.md:4458:🟡 **F-1081-2 — TWO MORE TESTS JOIN THE LOAD-FLAKY SET.** `e2e/e1-night-shift.spec.ts:413` ("lantern coverage is necessary for threat visibility") and `e2e/e1-baron.spec.ts:343` ("contract board requires science plus two secured claims…") each failed in **exactly one of four s1081 batteries**, with the graft held constant: 413 failed at 5-spec load and passed at 2-spec load; 343 did the reverse. A moving failure under a fixed cause is flake, not regression (the F-1078-2 / gate-battery-contention class). 413's failing read was `spriteLuminance >= 0.35, got 0.219 / 0.275` — a luminance sample, so heavy parallel GPU load is the plausible mechanism. ➡️ Worth a stabilisation rung (isolate or single-worker these two); **no corrective queued**, and until then a drain that trips either should re-run it isolated before believing it.
tasks/BACKLOG.md:4560:🟢 **F-1107-2 DOWNGRADED s1114 — `055-baron-kill-stop.spec.ts:121` DID **NOT** REPRODUCE, 8/8, AND ITS FOUNDING PREMISE ("load-INdependent") NOW LOOKS LIKE THE CONTENTION IT WAS DEFINED AGAINST.** ✓ VERIFIED by execution on a quiet box (loadavg 1.93 → 2.6), every run at `--workers=1`, the owed investigation finally taken. **Isolated (`:121` alone): 5/5 PASS at 13–14s** against a `test.setTimeout(45_000)` budget — ~31s of headroom, not a test scraping its ceiling. **Whole file (4 tests, so `:121` runs with its siblings' accumulated browser state): 3/3 PASS at 33.7 / 34.4 / 34.1s.** F-1107-2 was founded on a single red measured at `w1` during s1107's 43-test calibration sequence, and reasoned *"it failed under w1, the quietest condition in the entire sequence, therefore it is not a contention artefact."* **That inference does not survive F-1113-4**, which showed a guard the slice never touched going red on contention alone: `--workers=1` bounds *playwright's own* parallelism, it does not make the box quiet, and a long battery is not a quiet condition merely because it is serial. ⚖️ **HONEST STATISTICAL LIMIT, stated rather than buried: 8/8 green does not close it.** At F-1107-2's own implied in-battery rate (~1 in 4), eight clean runs would occur by luck ~10% of the time. So this is a **downgrade from "genuine load-independent flake, mechanism undiagnosed" to "does not reproduce in isolation or in-file on a quiet box"** — not a closure. ✍️ **AUTHORED s1114 — `tasks/lane-cw-02-wrecker-target-premise.md` (goal leaf `cw-02-wrecker-target-premise`), QUEUED SAME FIRE to lane-a**, byte-identical to the committed master (sha256 `46497ff9…`), `^CODEX:` ✓ verified at **column 0 on line 9** of the *queued copy* (F-1088-4). Closes F-1114-3, the last red of the F-1109-1 → F-1112-1 chain. **The evidence chain is this fire's own measurements, not an inheritance.** ⚠️ **The master's most important content is what it FORBIDS, because two hypotheses are already dead and re-running them would burn the run:** raising `advanceSim` is a firewall violation (5× changed nothing) and "the engine mis-targets" is answered at source (`Enemy.ts:1040-1075`, `nearestBuilding`). **SCOPE 1 IS A MANDATORY MEASUREMENT THAT GATES THE FIX** — print every `build.hp` entry's world position and its distance to the west gate `(-46, 38)` — then branch: **(2a)** a lantern *is* nearer ⇒ the premise is stale, rewrite the assertion to express its **intent** (*the structure nearest the west gate takes exactly one hit*, derived at runtime, never named by index), re-deciding `:135` in the same edit since the same event falsifies it; **(2b)** no lantern is nearer ⇒ `nearestBuilding` picks a farther target, which is a real engine bug and a **lawful STOP to the owner's desk with zero `src/` changes**. 🛡️ **The sharpest clause is anti-cargo-cult, and it is stated as such: re-pointing the assertion at whatever happened to get hit is explicitly forbidden — that is writing the guard from the answer sheet, it would go green while proving nothing, and being unable to express the intent is itself a VALID STOP.** Baseline banked for the whole family at `--workers=1`: **3 passed / 1 failed (45.9s)**. LANE-SAFETY pre-proved s1114: `main..lane/m3` is the single commit this fire drained itself (`70ce6e50`), its two-dot `src/`+`e2e/` diff vs main is **EMPTY**, worktree clean, queue empty ⇒ safe dupe, the reset loses nothing.
tasks/BACKLOG.md:4562:🟠 **F-1114-3 — F-1109-1'S LAST RED IS *SOLVED*, AND IT WAS NEVER A TIMING PROBLEM: `cw-02-escort:134` ASSERTS THE WRECKER HITS A BEACON, AND THE WRECKER CORRECTLY HITS A **LANTERN POST** INSTEAD.** ✓ VERIFIED s1114 by instrumented probe **plus** the engine source, after re-measuring F-1109-1's list on current main: **3 passed / 1 failed** — `e2-incline`, `e2-pressure-garden`, `e2-trestle` are all **GREEN** since `b43b31ad`, and the only survivor is `cw-02-escort.spec.ts:134`, `Expected: 39 · Received: 40` (reproducing s1113's report exactly). **① IT IS NOT A STALE SIM BUDGET — I killed that hypothesis first by measurement, not by argument:** raising `advanceSim(8)` to `advanceSim(40)` (5×) changed **nothing**, still `40/40`. **② WHAT ACTUALLY HAPPENS, from an instrumented run** (probe returned the full `build.hp` table; spec restored byte-identical to main afterwards, ✓ verified): after the sabotage window, `enemiesAlive: 0`, **all six `sentry_beacon` sit at a pristine `40/40`**, hero at `100`, and **exactly one structure is damaged — `lantern_post` index 0 at `34/35`.** That is **precisely 1 point**, i.e. `wreck.damage: 1` landing **exactly once**, which is what `wreck.hitCooldown: 999` guarantees: **the wrecker gets one swing in the whole window, and something other than the beacon received it.** ③ **THE MECHANISM, ✓ VERIFIED AT SOURCE — `src/entities/Enemy.ts:1040-1075` (`updateWrecker`): a wrecker seeks `context.nearestBuilding(this.group.position)`, re-targeting on a timer. It does NOT hunt the hero.** So the target is decided purely by **which structure is nearest the west spawn gate** — and `:104` (passing) pins that gate at `{ edge: 'west', x: -46, z: 38 }`, while `PYLONS[2]`, the beacon the test names, is placed at `(-28, 8)` — **about 35 units away**. Any lantern post inside that radius wins the only swing. ➡️ **THE TEST'S PREMISE IS GEOMETRIC AND IT IS STALE: it assumes the index-2 beacon is the nearest building to the west gate, and some map furniture change made that false.** The engine is behaving exactly as written. ⚠️ **NOT a one-line fix, and that is why I did not author it: `:135` (`lanterns.every(hp === maxHp)`) is falsified by the SAME event**, so the two assertions must be re-decided together — and "re-point the assertion at whatever got hit" would be writing the guard from the answer sheet, which is the one thing this spec exists to prevent. 🔎 **A CLUE THE NEXT FIRE SHOULD NOT HAVE TO RE-DERIVE: the two `lantern_post` entries are NOT placed by the test** — `:107-110` places **only** the six `sentry_beacon`s — yet both lanterns appear in the run's `build.hp` table, so **the contract/map places them**, and they are **not** the town lanterns in `townLayout.ts:170-174` (those are town-scale, e.g. `(-5.8, 9.85)`, an order of magnitude away from this map's `(-46, 38)`). So the thing to date is a change in **`e3-canyon-works`'s own furniture**, not in the town layout. ✅ **THE NEXT FIRE CAN FINISH THIS CHEAPLY, and here is the exact first step: print the `lantern_post` world positions and compare them to `(-46, 38)` against `PYLONS[2] = (-28, 8)`.** If a lantern genuinely sits nearer the gate, the honest cure is to make the test assert what it actually means — *the nearest structure to the west gate takes exactly one wrecker hit* — rather than naming a building by index; that keeps the guard's strength while removing the geometric assumption. **If instead NO lantern is nearer, the finding inverts into a real `nearestBuilding` bug and belongs on the owner's desk.** Measure before authoring (s1092/s1095/s1105/this).
