TypeScript checking passed, but browser probes confirmed building-identity collisions and missing nighttime lighting in the new world renderer.

Full review comments:

- [P2] Include the building family in each replay work key — /Users/robin/Claude/Projects/Gold Rush/worktrees/lane-c/src/world/LanternWorldStage.ts:236-236
  When different building families share an index, these keys collide because `BuildSystem.hpDiagnostics()` numbers each family separately. Consequently, `previous` interpolates buildings from another building’s position, and `looseSprites` can collapse distinct works into one sprite. A browser probe with stationary sentry 0 at x=-10 and sluice 0 at x=10 incorrectly placed the sentry at x=0 halfway through interpolation. Include `work.id` in the key, consistent with the [per-family pool invariant](AGENTS.md#L29).

- [P2] Apply replay lighting state before updating the light rig — /Users/robin/Claude/Projects/Gold Rush/worktrees/lane-c/src/world/LanternWorldStage.ts:181-181
  For Night Shift and other day/night contracts, the world stage never calls `LightRig.setNightShift()`, so passing elapsed time to `update()` leaves daylight enabled throughout playback. At Night Shift wave 11, the browser reported darkness 0, sun intensity 2.35, and zero lantern light pools despite the contract requiring full darkness from wave 10. Unlike the previous tactical presentation, the default world view therefore misrepresents nighttime conditions. Apply the contract’s lighting state and relevant light sources using replay wave/time.
